WEBVTT

00:00:00.373 --> 00:00:01.166
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 1

00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:04.327
<v><b>Steve:</b></v> Mum and Dad came from very different families.

00:00:04.887 --> 00:00:08.460
Dad’s father was a garage mechanic, and his mother was a waitress.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.859
They were Welsh Catholics, very traditional.

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:16.789
Mum’s parents, on the other hand, were, er, very well-educated.

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:20.285
Her father was Burmese, he was an expert in Burmese music,

00:00:20.512 --> 00:00:23.009
and her mother was half Burmese and half English,

00:00:23.174 --> 00:00:27.090
and she’d been very active politically in the independence movement in Burma.

00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:30.624
So, with these two very different sets of parents,

00:00:31.129 --> 00:00:35.672
my mum and dad were quite different people, and I think they still are.

00:00:37.742 --> 00:00:39.798
<v><b>Bridget:</b></v> So, my parents are both doctors.

00:00:39.838 --> 00:00:42.751
In fact, the family mythology is that they met

00:00:42.797 --> 00:00:46.205
because they were both dissecting the same dead body.

00:00:46.995 --> 00:00:51.603
Mum had come to London in her early teens.

00:00:51.805 --> 00:00:54.902
She’d left Burma during the war and spent, er,

00:00:54.941 --> 00:01:01.438
several years in India before coming to the UK with her mother and her two sisters.

00:01:03.584 --> 00:01:04.287
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 2

00:01:05.109 --> 00:01:07.757
<v><b>Steve:</b></v> I was actually a bit embarrassed about Mum’s culture,

00:01:07.757 --> 00:01:10.657
because it was so different from everybody else at school.

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:15.697
So, the idea of having this, kind of, Burmese family –

00:01:16.103 --> 00:01:19.385
not that anybody really at school knew where Burma was –

00:01:19.699 --> 00:01:22.867
um, it was quite different.

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:28.957
<v><b>Bridget:</b></v> I wasn’t so much aware of Mum and Dad coming from different cultures

00:01:28.991 --> 00:01:33.344
as I was aware of the fact that Mum wasn’t white,

00:01:33.418 --> 00:01:38.830
and so she didn’t fit in, and, in that regard, neither did we.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.178
I think, in terms of culture, what I was more conscious of was,

00:01:43.225 --> 00:01:49.375
um, the fact that they came from very different educational and class backgrounds.

00:01:49.641 --> 00:01:55.099
So, my mum was really interested in literature, she loved music,

00:01:55.427 --> 00:01:59.916
and she used to listen to the cricket, and she wasn’t a Catholic.

00:02:00.573 --> 00:02:05.294
My dad didn’t have much time for literature, or music, or cricket.

00:02:05.527 --> 00:02:13.323
He was interested in rugby, pot boilers, and spy stories, and history.

00:02:15.266 --> 00:02:16.188
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 3

00:02:16.935 --> 00:02:20.291
<v><b>Steve:</b></v> As a child, I felt there were not many positive things

00:02:20.311 --> 00:02:22.784
about growing up in a multicultural family.

00:02:23.417 --> 00:02:27.308
We grew up in the 1970s, and it was a time when, I think,

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.571
racism was not challenged in schools,

00:02:31.803 --> 00:02:37.123
and so we were quite often the victims of bullying because of the fact that we looked different,

00:02:37.243 --> 00:02:40.613
because our mum was from Burma and physically

00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:43.593
we all take after our mum much more than our dad.

00:02:45.647 --> 00:02:50.865
<v><b>Bridget:</b></v> I wouldn’t really say – I wouldn’t really describe our family as a multicultural family,

00:02:51.397 --> 00:02:54.279
largely because there was no Burmese spoken.

00:02:54.312 --> 00:02:58.161
My mother had forgotten her Burmese when she went to India.

00:02:58.496 --> 00:03:04.106
I would rather describe our family as a mixed-race family.

00:03:04.548 --> 00:03:07.540
And, to be frank, that was quite tough.

00:03:07.626 --> 00:03:15.446
It was quite tough being in the 1970s, growing up in south Wales,

00:03:15.492 --> 00:03:22.727
which was a very white region of the country, um, and, you know,

00:03:22.758 --> 00:03:29.030
quite frankly, enduring a lot of, um, very explicit racism.

00:03:30.678 --> 00:03:32.673
So, that was quite a big downside.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.825
The upside, I think…well, the food.

00:03:36.011 --> 00:03:44.708
Um, but also, I think, that we grew up appreciative of other points of view.

00:03:44.761 --> 00:03:48.361
I think that we are all, in our different ways,

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:56.084
sensitive to when people don’t fit in and want to, kind of,

00:03:56.517 --> 00:04:05.439
help people through that and, um, yeah, and I actually think that that is a social benefit.

00:04:07.293 --> 00:04:08.222
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 4

00:04:08.988 --> 00:04:12.087
<v><b>Steve:</b></v> In spite of feeling very different when we were at school,

00:04:12.346 --> 00:04:18.908
our upbringing was, I think, very typical middle-class British family.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:22.751
Um, we watched television programmes,

00:04:22.776 --> 00:04:28.765
did the sorts of things that white middle-class British people do.

00:04:28.971 --> 00:04:33.419
Um, so there wasn’t much about the daily life

00:04:33.456 --> 00:04:36.645
 that was very different from what other kids at school were doing.

00:04:38.569 --> 00:04:44.137
<v><b>Bridget:</b></v> I think that, um, my sense of identity as a child

00:04:44.162 --> 00:04:49.708
was very much shaped by the fact that we were brought up in south Wales.

00:04:50.384 --> 00:04:55.231
So, there was very definitely an identity of, um,

00:04:55.251 --> 00:05:01.436
Welshness among our peers, as opposed to Englishness.

00:05:01.916 --> 00:05:06.498
And there is no question that Welshness at that time

00:05:06.931 --> 00:05:16.238
was really associated with being born in Wales and also, crudely, being white.

00:05:16.378 --> 00:05:23.338
So, we didn’t really have the option of being Welsh, um, or of being English,

00:05:23.598 --> 00:05:28.974
and I didn’t feel Burmese because of not speaking Burmese.

00:05:29.938 --> 00:05:34.240
So, I remember talking to my grandmother about it, and –

00:05:34.285 --> 00:05:40.372
and whether she felt British or English – and she said, ‘I just feel me.’

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:49.152
And, I guess, that’s the same as I’ve felt and feel. I just feel ‘me’.

00:05:51.034 --> 00:05:51.982
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 5

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:56.536
<v><b>Steve:</b></v> Of course, where there was a difference with the other kids at school

00:05:56.755 --> 00:05:58.408
was in the food that we ate,

00:05:58.935 --> 00:06:07.398
because Mum loved to cook and she would make all sorts of interesting dishes with spices.

00:06:07.485 --> 00:06:10.740
And, of course, even though it was all delicious,

00:06:10.793 --> 00:06:14.787
again we felt a little bit embarrassed going to school the next day

00:06:14.939 --> 00:06:21.001
if our clothes smelled of spices, or if our breath smelled of garlic.

00:06:21.193 --> 00:06:23.441
And when we took friends home from school,

00:06:23.967 --> 00:06:28.518
I was quite keen for Mum to make fish fingers and chips,

00:06:28.584 --> 00:06:34.261
and not delicious homemade curry because I didn’t want people, kind of,

00:06:34.615 --> 00:06:37.525
making fun of me because we were eating funny food.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:44.122
<v><b>Bridget:</b></v> I think one aspect of my life that was different was

00:06:44.147 --> 00:06:51.510
that I was really surrounded in the family by very high-achieving women.

00:06:51.941 --> 00:06:55.530
Which isn’t to say that I felt pressure from that, um,

00:06:55.683 --> 00:07:08.089
it was just normal for women to go to work as professionals and – and to be a mum.

00:07:08.597 --> 00:07:13.948
And, I just took that for granted and now, looking back, I realize that,

00:07:13.974 --> 00:07:16.997
actually, that was quite unusual.

00:07:18.886 --> 00:07:19.873
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 6

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.589
<v><b>Steve:</b></v> I think that my English and Burmese, er,

00:07:23.614 --> 00:07:26.539
heritage has influenced what I do today, very much.

00:07:26.951 --> 00:07:35.540
My restaurant, Ma Khin Café, er, has Burmese food, um, very real Burmese food, um,

00:07:35.746 --> 00:07:38.790
lots of dishes that you would actually eat if you went to Burma today,

00:07:38.924 --> 00:07:42.667
 and also, er, food from the countries that are around Burma,

00:07:42.714 --> 00:07:46.628
and that have influenced Burmese cuisine, because, like all cuisines,

00:07:47.029 --> 00:07:52.891
Burma’s food culture has developed through the influence of the countries that surround it,

00:07:53.078 --> 00:07:57.231
the people that have travelled to live there, the people that have invaded it,

00:07:57.271 --> 00:07:58.958
the people that have occupied it.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:09.392
<v><b>Bridget:</b></v> Being half Burmese and half British –  I wouldn’t say English –

00:08:10.125 --> 00:08:14.022
um, has definitely influenced what I do today.

00:08:14.047 --> 00:08:18.070
In fact, I think, really, in some ways you could say I’ve built my whole life around it.

00:08:18.644 --> 00:08:27.963
So, I’m now an academic at the University of Bristol and I’m a professor of migration and citizenship,

00:08:28.385 --> 00:08:35.462
so my research is all around questions of migration, race, and belonging,

00:08:35.486 --> 00:08:37.642
so you could say it’s my life work.

00:08:38.102 --> 00:08:44.120
And, of course, it’s deeply influenced the book that I’ve put together with my brother, Steve.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:48.883
Nobody else could have written that book, that book came from, yeah,

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:53.571
growing up as mixed-race children in south Wales.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:09:01.945
In that respect it’s as much about our history as it is about our grandmother and Burmese history.